  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Consider the graphs of \\(y=2\\log x\\) and \\(y=\\log2x.\\) We may say that:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "they do not intersect"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "they intersect at \\(1\\) point only"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "they intersect at \\(2\\) points only"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "they intersect at a finite number of points but greater than\n\\(2\\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "they coincide"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "b",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1961-p19.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "On the domain \\(x>0,\\) equality requires \\[ \\log(x^2)=\\log(2x), \\] so \\(x^2=2x.\\) The only positive solution is \\(x=2,\\) giving exactly one intersection.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B.",
      "hints": [
        "Use \\(2\\log x=\\log(x^2)\\) for \\(x>0\\)",
        "Equate the logarithm arguments and respect the logarithm’s domain"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
